<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

	<PropertyGroup>
		<TargetFrameworks>net8.0;net9.0;net10.0</TargetFrameworks>
		<LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
		<ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
		<Nullable>enable</Nullable>
		<PackageId>EventFlux.RabbitFlow</PackageId>
		<Version>1.3.0</Version>
		<Authors>Kadir Demirkaya</Authors>
		<PackageLicenseFile>LICENSE.txt</PackageLicenseFile>
		<Description>Lightweight .NET library for event-driven applications, providing distributed event support and domain events with EventFlux and RabbitMQ.</Description>
		<RepositoryType>git</RepositoryType>
		<RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/kadirdemirkaya/EventFlux.RabbitFlow</RepositoryUrl>
		<PackageProjectUrl>https://github.com/kadirdemirkaya/EventFlux.RabbitFlow</PackageProjectUrl>
		<PackageTags>
			CQRS;EventBus;EventSourcing;DistributedEvents;RabbitMQ;EventFlux;DomainEvents;.NET;MessageBroker;EventDriven
		</PackageTags>
		<PackageReleaseNotes>v1.3.0:
			- Publishing reuses channels from a small pool instead of opening and closing one per message (about 5x faster sequential publishing, 2-3x with publisher confirms)
			- Unsubscribe keeps the event queue and the messages waiting in it (unbinds it and cancels its consumer); the previous deletion is available with RabbitFlowOptions.DeleteQueueOnUnsubscribe
			- Graceful shutdown: consumers stop first and messages being handled finish and are acknowledged before the channel closes, so they are no longer handled twice after a restart. Added RabbitFlowOptions.ShutdownTimeout (30 s) for disposal without AutoSubscribe
			- EventBusSubscriptionsManager is thread safe: concurrent subscriptions no longer make every message of an event fail, handler lists are snapshots, and an unknown event returns no handlers instead of throwing
			- A stopped broker no longer resumes consuming after a channel is closed
			- Message format unchanged; no API removed

			v1.2.0:
			- Connecting no longer blocks a thread: added IPersistenceConnection.TryConnectAsync(CancellationToken) (default implementation calls TryConnect), used by publish and subscribe. The token passed to PublishAsync also cancels waiting for the broker, and disposing the connection stops a reconnect in progress
			- Fixed message loss after a broker restart without automatic recovery: exchanges, queues, bindings and dead-letter queues are declared again before consumers restart
			- Fixed consumers never restarting after the broker closed the consumer channel while automatic recovery was enabled (the default)
			- Consumer restart is retried with back-off while the broker is unreachable instead of giving up after one failure
			- Added RabbitFlowOptions.MaxDeliveryAttempts and RedeliveryDelay (opt-in): a failing message is retried a limited number of times, optionally after a delay, then moved to the dead-letter queue. Without them nothing changes
			- Added RabbitFlowOptions.PublisherConfirms (opt-in): PublishAsync completes after the broker confirms the message; rejected messages are retried and then throw, events without a bound queue are logged
			- Fixed message loss in DeadLetter mode: dead-letter publishes are confirmed by the broker before the original message is acknowledged, and requeued if they cannot be routed
			- Added IPersistenceConnection.CreateChannelAsync(CreateChannelOptions, CancellationToken) with a default implementation
			- No breaking changes; message format unchanged

			v1.1.0:
			- Migrated to EventFlux 2.1.0 (requires EventFlux &gt;= 2.1.0). Incoming messages are dispatched with IEventBus.PublishAsync(IEventRequest, CancellationToken); the MissingMethodException that made 1.0.3 drop every message under EventFlux 2.x is gone
			- Event handlers now use the EventFlux 2.x signature Handle(TEvent, CancellationToken); the token is the delivery's cancellation token
			- Targets net8.0, net9.0 and net10.0 (net6.0 and net7.0 were dropped because EventFlux 2.x does not support them). Microsoft.Extensions.* dependencies follow the major version of each target, so net8 apps no longer hit CS1705 or assembly load errors
			- New failure semantics: a message whose handler throws is no longer acknowledged. By default it is rejected and requeued (RabbitFlowOptions.OnFailure = Requeue); OnFailure = DeadLetter moves it to a dead-letter queue instead. Messages are never lost on handler failure
			- IEventBroker, IPersistenceConnection and IEventBusSubscriptionsManager are singletons, so subscriptions live until the host stops instead of ending with the scope that created them
			- Added RabbitFlowOptions and an AddEventFluxRabbitFlow overload to configure them (retry count, failure handling, dead-letter names, prefetch, auto subscription)
			- Added RabbitFlowOptions.AutoSubscribe, which subscribes every handler in the scanned assembly when the host starts, and made IServiceProvider.UseEventFluxRabbitFlow public
			- Added CancellationToken overloads to IEventBroker.PublishAsync
			- Subscribing the same handler twice, or to a second exchange, no longer throws
			- Publish retries now cover the whole publish call and keep the original exception
			- Connection recovery: with automatic recovery enabled (the default) the client's own recovery is no longer interrupted by a second connection; with it disabled the connection is replaced, the old one released and consumers are restarted. Blocked connections and callback exceptions no longer trigger a reconnect, and concurrent connect calls open a single connection
			- Publishing while the connection is recovering is retried instead of failing immediately
			- Removed the "throw-fake-exception" test hook from the consumer
			- Log messages are in English and use structured templates
			- Message format is unchanged: same JSON bodies, routing key = event type name, same exchange and queue names</PackageReleaseNotes>
		<AssemblyInformationalVersion>1.3.0</AssemblyInformationalVersion>
		<PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
		<PackageRequireLicenseAcceptance>false</PackageRequireLicenseAcceptance>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<None Include="..\..\README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="" />
		<None Include="..\..\LICENSE.txt" Pack="true" PackagePath="" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<InternalsVisibleTo Include="EventFlux.RabbitFlow.Tests" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<PackageReference Include="EventFlux" Version="2.1.0" />
		<PackageReference Include="Newtonsoft.Json" Version="13.0.3" />
		<PackageReference Include="Polly" Version="8.5.2" />
		<PackageReference Include="RabbitMQ.Client" Version="7.1.2" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup Condition="'$(TargetFramework)' == 'net8.0'">
		<PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection.Abstractions" Version="8.0.2" />
		<PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Abstractions" Version="8.0.3" />
		<PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting.Abstractions" Version="8.0.1" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup Condition="'$(TargetFramework)' == 'net9.0'">
		<PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection.Abstractions" Version="9.0.20" />
		<PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Abstractions" Version="9.0.20" />
		<PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting.Abstractions" Version="9.0.20" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup Condition="'$(TargetFramework)' == 'net10.0'">
		<PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection.Abstractions" Version="10.0.12" />
		<PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Abstractions" Version="10.0.12" />
		<PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting.Abstractions" Version="10.0.12" />
	</ItemGroup>

</Project>
